  "Question": "Let \\(D=a^2+b^2+c^2,\\) where \\(a,\\) \\(b\\) are consecutive integers and \\(c=ab.\\) Then \\(\\sqrt D\\) is:",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "always an even integer",
    "B": "sometimes an odd integer, sometimes not",
    "C": "always an odd integer",
    "D": "sometimes rational, sometimes not",
    "E": "always irrational"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "C",
  "Solution": "Consecutive integers differ by 1, so squaring their difference connects their squares with their product:\n\\[\n(a-b)^2=1\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad a^2+b^2=2ab+1.\n\\]\nUsing \\(c=ab\\), we obtain\n\\[\nD=a^2+b^2+(ab)^2\n=(ab)^2+2ab+1\n=(ab+1)^2.\n\\]\nTherefore \\(\\sqrt D=|ab+1|\\). One of two consecutive integers is even, so \\(ab\\) is even and \\(ab+1\\) is odd. Taking an absolute value preserves oddness, including when negative integers are allowed. Hence \\(\\sqrt D\\) is always an odd integer, choice C.",
